    from jsonb_populate_record(null::public.listening_items, $seed${"slug":"b2-technical-interview-for-a-backend-role","title":"Interview: The Bug That Charged Customers Twice","level":"B2","status":"published","sort_order":200,"metadata":{"format":"dialogue","script_type":"dialogue","speakers":["Daniel","Kamila"]},"summary":"In a job interview for a backend developer role, a candidate explains a difficult bug, how she found it and what she learned.","transcript_text":"Daniel: Thanks for coming in, Kamila. I'd like to start with something practical. Can you tell me about a bug that you found really difficult to fix?\nKamila: Sure, so last year I worked on the payment service for an online shop. Every few days, a small number of customers were charged twice for the same order.\nDaniel: That sounds stressful. How did you approach it?\nKamila: Well, at first we couldn't reproduce it at all. Everything worked perfectly on our test servers. So I added more detailed logging and waited for it to happen again.\nDaniel: And what did the logs show?\nKamila: They showed that when the network was slow, the mobile app sent the payment request a second time. Our server treated the two requests as two separate orders.\nDaniel: Right, so it was a retry problem. How did you fix it?\nKamila: We gave every payment request a unique key. If the server receives the same key twice, it simply returns the first result instead of charging the customer again.\nDaniel: Good, and looking back, what would you do differently?\nKamila: Honestly, I'd test for slow and unreliable networks from the start. We'd only tested the happy path, where everything goes right. I also learned to tell the support team about a problem early, so they can reassure customers while we're working on it.\nDaniel: That's a really useful lesson. Okay, let's move on to some questions about databases.","category":"Technology","accent":"general","speed_label":"natural","speed_wpm":150,"duration_seconds":93,"audio_url":null,"focus_skill":"detail","tags":["job interview","IT","dialogue","problem solving"],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What was the problem with the payment service?","options":["It was too slow for mobile users.","Some customers paid twice for one order.","Customers could not log in to the shop.","Orders were sent to the wrong address."],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Kamila says: \"a small number of customers were charged twice for the same order.\""},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why was the bug hard to fix at first?","options":["The logs had been deleted.","The mobile app was not finished.","The team could not make it happen on their test servers.","Customers did not report it."],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"\"At first we couldn't reproduce it at all. Everything worked perfectly on our test servers.\""},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"What caused the double payments?","options":["A mistake in the shop's prices","The server crashing at night","Customers clicking the button twice","The app sending the request again on a slow network"],"answer_index":3,"explanation":"\"When the network was slow, the mobile app sent the payment request a second time.\""},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"How did Kamila's fix work?","options":["Repeated requests with the same key return the first result.","The app stopped working on slow networks.","Customers had to confirm every payment by phone.","The server deleted every second order."],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"\"If the server receives the same key twice, it simply returns the first result instead of charging the customer again.\""},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does Kamila's last answer suggest about her?","options":["She blames the support team for the problem.","She learns from mistakes and thinks about the customers.","She prefers to work alone.","She thinks testing is a waste of time."],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"She would test unreliable networks \"from the start\" and tell support early \"so they can reassure customers\"."}]}$seed$::jsonb) r
